            <ArticleTitle>Female Runner With Painful Left Thigh Swelling: A Case of May-Thurner Syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-1482(17)30671-8</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmrj.2017.06.016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We discuss a case of a female runner who presented with burning left groin and thigh pain and positional swelling after a long run. The workup revealed May-Thurner syndrome. May-Thurner syndrome anatomy is a common anatomical variant present in approximately 20% of the population. Compression of the iliac vein may result in venous stasis in the lower limb with a predisposition to deep venous thrombosis. We identify 4 reported cases of May-Thurner syndrome in female runners and review the importance of diagnosis and appropriate treatment of this condition.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">To be determined.</AbstractText>
